HALLOW_PAGE = [
("lore", "Norse Wheel of the Year", "Hallowing: the hammer sign and fire",
 ["To hallow something is to set it apart as sacred. The old texts show two ways. In Hákonar saga góða, King Hákon is a Christian who is pressed to drink at a heathen feast. He makes the sign of the cross over the cup, and the angry farmers are told that the king is only doing what everyone does: blessing the cup in Þórr’s name with the sign of the hammer.",
  "The other way was fire. In the Landnámabók, early settlers in Iceland claimed land by carrying fire around it, and in Eyrbyggja saga Þórólfr Mostrarskegg carries fire around the land he claims. The fire marked a line between what was his and what was not.",
  "Both can be done at home. To hallow a cup before a toast, hold your hand over it and draw the shape of a hammer in the air, say the name of who you are honoring, and then drink. To hallow a room or a yard, carry a lit candle along its edges, saying aloud that this place is yours and will be kept well. Put the flame out when you are done.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The hammer sign is in Hákonar saga góða in Snorri’s Heimskringla. The fire claim is in the Landnámabók and Eyrbyggja saga. The two practices above are modern, built on those two reports."],
 "What will you hallow first: a cup, a doorway, a room? Name it and the words you will say."),
]

DEAD_PAGE = [
("lore", "Ancestors", "Waking the dead",
 ["The old poems know the dead can be woken. In the Hervararkviða, Hervǫr sails to an island of burial mounds. At night, with fire burning over the mounds, she stands before her father Angantýr’s mound and calls him up. She asks for the sword that lies buried with him. He warns her away, and she does not back down. At last he gives it to her.",
  "In the Grógaldr, a young man named Svipdagr stands at his dead mother Gróa’s mound and asks her to wake and sing him charms for a hard road. She sings nine of them.",
  "Both scenes follow one pattern: go to the mound, call the dead by name, and ask for one clear thing. In both, the dead may warn you or refuse, and the living need nerve and a good reason.",
  "A modern way to follow the pattern does not need a graveyard or darkness. Sit with a grave, a photograph, or something that belonged to the person. Say their name aloud. Ask for one thing: advice, strength, or a story. Then be quiet and listen. Thank them, and leave something small. The dead may not answer in words.",
  "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Hervararkviða is in Hervarar saga ok Heiðreks. The Grógaldr is the first poem of the Svipdagsmál, kept in later paper manuscripts. The practice above is modern."],
 "Who would you call, and what is the one thing you would ask? Write the name and the question."),
]

